@meta
id: 04-16
title: Three jobs, one file, no scrolling
chapter: 04
type: lesson
skills: count-prefix, relativenumber, hjkl
par: 148
minutes: 8
vscode: scroll, click, type, scroll, select, copy, paste, paste
open: payout.fixture.ts
vimrc: set relativenumber number

@brief
Everything in this chapter, used together, on one file of test fixtures.
Three jobs:

  1. The last entry in the array, `PAY-4127`, has no trailing comma.
     Add one.

  2. Exactly one entry has `status: 'PENDING'`. Append
     ` // TODO settle before the next export` to the end of that line —
     a space, two slashes, a space, then the text.

  3. The load test needs four more placeholder entries, after the last
     real one and before the closing `];`, each reading exactly:

         { id: 'PAY-0000', merchantId: 'MER-00', amount: 0, status: 'PENDING' },

     They are indented two spaces like every other entry. Vim will supply
     that indent for you when it opens the lines, so type the text from
     the brace onwards and let it.

Job three is a count on an insert command, not four trips through the same
typing. Job two is above job one, so one of your jumps goes upward.

Plan the order before you touch the keyboard. There is a route through
this that never reads a margin number twice and never presses a motion
key without a count in front of it.

@keys
| `30j` | down thirty lines |
| `6k` | up six lines |
| `4o` | open four lines, repeating what you type on each |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Comma first, then the TODO above it, then the new entries — or top down,
the TODO first. Either works. Bouncing between them does not.
--
The comma is on the last entry, one line above `];`. Read its distance
off the margin.
--
After the comma, the `PENDING` entry is six lines up: `6k`, then `A`, then
the comment. Then back down to the last entry and `4o`, the placeholder
typed once, `Esc`.

@solution
    30j A , <Esc>
    6k A  // TODO settle before the next export <Esc>
    6j 4o { id: 'PAY-0000', merchantId: 'MER-00', amount: 0, status: 'PENDING' }, <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Around a hundred and thirty keystrokes, a hundred and fifteen of which
are text you had to type anyway. The navigation cost eleven.

Count the alternative honestly. In an editor with a mouse this is two
scrolls, four clicks, a selection, a copy and three pastes, and the pastes
are the part most likely to go wrong — paste three times, check you got
four, find you have five. `4o` cannot produce five.

That is the whole argument for this chapter. Not that `30j` is faster than
thirty taps of an arrow key, though it is. It is that a count is a
statement of intent that either lands where you said or does not happen at
all, and holding a key down is a guess you have to check afterwards.

